WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
Some secure configuration failures appear again and again.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:07.000
Debug mode in production can expose internals.

3
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:15.180
Verbose error messages can reveal stack traces, file paths, environment details, or downstream service names.

4
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.720
Default credentials can leave administrative interfaces open to anyone who knows the vendor default.

5
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.660
Missing audit logging can make security events harder to investigate.

6
00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.220
Cloud and web settings are frequent sources of risk.

7
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.400
Public storage buckets can expose data.

8
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:37.060
Overly permissive CORS can allow unintended web origins to interact with browser-accessible API responses.

9
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.200
Weak cookie settings can make session protection worse.

10
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.620
Disabled TLS verification can weaken the application's ability to confirm the identity of remote services.

11
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.680
These are often one-line settings with large consequences.

12
00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.080
Infrastructure exposure is another common pattern.

13
00:00:53.180 --> 00:01:02.780
Open management ports, permissive firewall rules, broad security groups, public dashboards, and default administrative interfaces create opportunities for misuse.

14
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:07.440
Excessive IAM permissions can let a service do far more than it needs.

15
00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:15.060
When these settings are buried in cloud consoles or copied templates, teams may not notice the overexposure until an incident or audit.

16
00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:19.820
Feature flags and runtime switches also need security review.

17
00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:29.680
A flag that bypasses validation, disables authorization, changes data visibility, or enables an experimental integration can be as sensitive as code.

18
00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:35.860
Unsafe feature flags should have owners, expiration, monitoring, and rollout plans.

19
00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:43.480
Configuration failures are often simple, but their impact can be serious because they change how secure code is allowed to behave.
